@[grub page]
Setting up GRUB takes three commands in the new system (details below):
  1. @[grub-install ...]        put GRUB on the disk / EFI partition
  2. check /etc/default/grub  (root=PARTUUID, extra kernel options)
  3. @[grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg]   write the boot menu
GRUB comes from the @[grub2] cache (spell-caches step); it supports both BIOS
and UEFI. How this machine booted the ISO:
  @[ls /sys/firmware/efi]  # exists: UEFI; "No such file": BIOS

First enter the new system: @[enchantment chroot]

1. Install GRUB
 BIOS: to the disk's boot sector (the whole disk, not a partition):
  @[grub-install --target=i386-pc /dev/sda]
  (a GPT disk needs a small "BIOS boot" partition without a filesystem)
 UEFI: the EFI system partition must be mounted at /boot/efi (do that before
 the chroot: @[mount /dev/sda1 $ENCHANT_TARGET/boot/efi]; @[efibootmgr] lists
 the firmware's boot entries):
 @[grub-install --target=x86_64-efi --efi-directory=/boot/efi --bootloader-id=smgl]
 If the firmware doesn't offer it at boot, add the fallback loader too:
  @[grub-install --target=x86_64-efi --efi-directory=/boot/efi --removable]

2. Check /etc/default/grub
 Without an initramfs the kernel finds its root filesystem by device name or
 PARTUUID, not by filesystem UUID, so these two lines must be there:
  GRUB_DISABLE_LINUX_UUID=true
  GRUB_DISABLE_LINUX_PARTUUID=false
 (older grub2 caches have a misspelled GRUB_DISABLE_UUID=true instead.)
 Kernel options you needed for this ISO (e.g. fbcon=rotate:1 for a sideways
 screen) go into GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT="..." here as well.

3. Write the boot menu (it lists the kernels in /boot, so do this after the
 kernel step, and again after installing another kernel):
  @[grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg]
 and check that the "linux" lines say root=PARTUUID=... or root=/dev/...:
  @[grep 'linux.*root=' /boot/grub/grub.cfg]

Leave the new system: @[exit]

Type @[next] to go on to the user setup (or @[todo] for the boot-loader step).
